/**
 * Problem spoke — "fleas are still here after I treated the pet."
 *
 * THE ARGUMENT: the pupal window. The cocoon is resistant to insecticide, the
 * adult inside waits for a host cue, and so fleas keep appearing for weeks
 * after a correct treatment. Kentucky supplies the expectation-setting line
 * and the four-week retreatment threshold.
 *
 * VERIFIED — the pupal window (this is the core of the page):
 *  - Univ. of Kentucky Entfact-602: "The cocoon is also impervious to
 *    insecticides—another reason some fleas may persist for an extended
 *    period."; "Even after treatment, expect to see some fleas for a few weeks
 *    or longer. These are often newly emerged adults." Retreatment if fleas
 *    continue "beyond 4 weeks".
 *  - AgriLife citybugs ENT-3001: "The pupal stage normally lasts 7-14 days, but
 *    can persist for much longer under certain conditions. The pupa is normally
 *    well-protected from the effects of pesticide sprays and is very difficult
 *    to kill with insecticides."; "Fleas that are in the pupal stage when
 *    insecticides are applied, frequently survive treatment to emerge several
 *    days later."
 *  - UC IPM: "At cool temperatures, fully formed fleas can remain in their
 *    cocoons for up to 12 months."; "warm temperatures and mechanical pressure
 *    caused by walking on or vacuuming carpet stimulate emergence from the
 *    cocoon."; "Vacuuming is very effective in killing larvae in the carpet,
 *    picking up adults, and stimulating preemerged adults to leave their
 *    cocoons."; "Flea larvae are unlikely to survive in areas with exposure to
 *    sunlight or substantial foot traffic."
 *  - CDC: "The cocoon protects pupa from environmental conditions and
 *    insecticides/repellents for several days or weeks."; "Adult fleas will not
 *    emerge from the cocoon until there is a clear presence of a host, such as
 *    movement or body heat."
 *  - Texas A&M Livestock Veterinary Entomology: pre-emergent adults "can remain
 *    in an abandoned room, barn or facility for up to 5 months."; "The release
 *    of CO2 by a host triggers the adults to emerge from the pupa cases."
 *  - Ohio State: emergence signals "may include vibrations (pet and people
 *    movement), pressure (host animal lying down on them), heat, noise, or
 *    carbon dioxide."
 *  - Species: cat flea Ctenocephalides felis is "the most common ectoparasite
 *    on domestic cats and dogs" (UC IPM); AgriLife: "Adult cat fleas feed on
 *    dogs, cats, and a variety of furred animals."; Texas A&M Livestock Vet
 *    Entomology: it "will feed on cats, humans, domestic dogs, and several
 *    livestock species, as well as opossums and raccoons."
 *  - Egg output: Kentucky "40-50 per day" on the pet with "hundreds or
 *    thousands of eggs, larvae, and pupae elsewhere in the home or yard."
 *  - Vacuuming: Kentucky — "vacuuming stimulates pre-adult fleas to emerge
 *    sooner from their insecticide-resistant cocoons, thus hastening their
 *    contact with insecticide residues". AgriLife's own removal figure: "15-30%
 *    of larvae and 30-60% of flea eggs from carpeting."
 *  - Yard: OSU — "Open areas of the lawn exposed to continuous sunlight won't
 *    support flea development, but shady, moist areas will."; UGA — "spraying
 *    the entire yard is wasteful"; AgriLife — focus on "crawl spaces, bedding
 *    areas, sites under decks and shrubbery, and shady areas wherever your pets
 *    spend a lot of time."; UC IPM — treating the pet with a preferred product
 *    "normally will prevent reinfestation."
 *  - Typhus, DSHS: over 8,600 cases 2008–2025, max 1,110 (2025), min 135
 *    (2010); "Approximately 72% of the cases were hospitalized and 17
 *    fatalities were attributed to the disease."; concentrated "in South Texas
 *    from Nueces County southward to the Rio Grande Valley" with new endemic
 *    areas in DFW, Greater Houston, Bexar and Travis. Incubation 6–14 days.
 *  - CDC on the mechanism: "Fleas poop when they feed. This poop (also called
 *    flea dirt) contains the bacteria, which can be rubbed into the bite wound
 *    or other wounds and cause an infection." — NOT the bite itself.
 *
 * ⚠️ FIVE HONESTY FLAGS THAT MUST SURVIVE ANY EDIT:
 *  1. THE "5% ADULTS / 95% OTHER STAGES" FIGURE IS NOT VERIFIED. Checked in 11
 *     extension and CDC sources; it appears in none of them. It likely comes
 *     from confusion with OSU's DIFFERENT, spatial claim that 95% of the
 *     infestation is in 5% of the house or yard. This page does not use either
 *     number as a life-stage proportion.
 *  2. PUPAL DORMANCY FIGURES RANGE ENORMOUSLY AND MUST NOT BE BLENDED: 7–14
 *     days typical (AgriLife), 2–4 weeks (Kentucky PSEP), 5 days to 5 weeks
 *     (UF), 140–170 days (OSU), up to 5 months (Texas A&M Livestock), up to 12
 *     months at cool temperatures (UC IPM). Give the typical figure and the
 *     documented extremes, attributed, and say the sources differ.
 *  3. VACUUMING REMOVAL RATES CONFLICT THREE WAYS: 15–30% of larvae and 30–60%
 *     of eggs (AgriLife), "very effective in killing larvae" (UC IPM), "up to
 *     95 percent of the flea eggs" (Ohio State). Use AgriLife's figure with
 *     attribution; do not present any of them as the settled number.
 *  4. NO LOCAL TYPHUS RISK MAY BE IMPLIED. The DSHS 2008–2019 county table
 *     shows El Paso as the ONLY West Texas county, with ONE case (2013). The
 *     burden is South Texas — Hidalgo 1,053, Nueces 667, Bexar 425, Cameron
 *     388. The page must state the disease exists and that this region is not
 *     where it is happening.
 *  5. CAT FLEAS TRANSMITTING PLAGUE IN WEST TEXAS IS NOT VERIFIED. DSHS
 *     confirms plague in wild rodents across "the western two-thirds of Texas"
 *     and that human plague is rare, with no Texas human case count on that
 *     page. Plague is therefore NOT discussed on this page at all rather than
 *     discussed speculatively.
 */
