<BaseLayout
  title="Still Fleas After Treating the Pet | Trusted Termite"
  description="The cocoon resists insecticide and waits for a host cue, which is why fleas keep appearing after a correct treatment — and when four weeks becomes the threshold."
  path="/services/flea-and-tick/fleas-after-treating-the-pet/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Flea and tick · problem page</p>
      <h1>Still fleas, after the pet was treated</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          This is the call that makes people lose faith in pest control, and almost always for a reason
          that has nothing to do with the quality of the work. The pet was treated. The house was treated.
          Ten days later somebody gets bitten in the front room, and the obvious conclusion is that
          somebody was sold something that did not work.
        </p>
        <p>
          The actual explanation is a life stage. Texas A&amp;M states that the flea pupa is normally
          well protected from the effects of pesticide sprays and is very difficult to kill with
          insecticides, and that fleas in the pupal stage when insecticides are applied frequently survive
          treatment to emerge several days later. Kentucky's extension service is even shorter about it:
          the cocoon is impervious to insecticides.
        </p>
        <p>
          So a correct treatment does not end the problem on the day it happens, and any company that lets
          you believe otherwise has set you up to be disappointed. Kentucky publishes the expectation
          instead: after treatment, expect to see some fleas for a few weeks or longer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The cocoon, which is the whole story</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>What the pupa actually is.</strong> A larva spins a cocoon and completes development
          inside it. The adult flea forms within that case and then — this is the important part — waits.
          It does not emerge on a timer. It emerges on a signal.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why insecticide does not reach it.</strong> The cocoon is a physical barrier. The CDC
          states that it protects the pupa from environmental conditions and from insecticides and
          repellents for several days or weeks. Texas A&amp;M's own wording is that the pupa is normally
          well protected from the effects of pesticide sprays and very difficult to kill. Kentucky's is
          that the cocoon is impervious to them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>How long it can wait, with the sources disagreeing openly.</strong> Texas A&amp;M gives
          the pupal stage as normally seven to fourteen days but able to persist much longer under certain
          conditions. Kentucky's pesticide programme gives two to four weeks, sometimes longer. Florida
          gives five days to five weeks in the absence of hosts. Oklahoma State says pre-emerged adults can
          lie dormant for 140 to 170 days. Texas A&amp;M's livestock entomology group says they can remain
          in an abandoned room, barn or facility for up to five months. UC IPM says that at cool
          temperatures, fully formed fleas can remain in their cocoons for up to twelve months.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We are not going to average those.</strong> Two weeks is the typical figure and it is the
          one to plan around. The long figures are real, they come from good sources, and they explain the
          cases that otherwise look impossible — a house shut up for the summer, a rent house that stood
          empty, a barn nobody has used since spring.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What triggers emergence.</strong> A host. The CDC states that adults will not emerge
          until there is a clear presence of one, such as movement or body heat. UC IPM names warm
          temperatures and the mechanical pressure of walking on or vacuuming carpet. Ohio State lists
          vibrations from pets and people, pressure from an animal lying on them, heat, noise and carbon
          dioxide. Texas A&amp;M's livestock group specifies carbon dioxide released by a host.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Which explains the most alarming version of this.</strong> A property that has been empty
          — a rental between tenants, a house after a death in the family, a place shut up while somebody
          worked away — can produce a wave of fleas within minutes of somebody walking in. Nothing hatched
          because of the visit; a population of fully formed adults had been waiting for exactly that cue.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Where the population actually is</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Not mostly on the animal.</strong> Kentucky describes adult fleas laying forty to fifty
          eggs a day in the pet's fur, with hundreds or thousands of eggs, larvae and pupae elsewhere in
          the home or yard. The eggs are not sticky and they fall off wherever the animal goes.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A note on a number you will see everywhere.</strong> There is a very widely quoted claim
          that only five percent of a flea population is adults and ninety-five percent is other stages. We
          went looking for it in eleven extension and public health sources and could not find it in any of
          them. What does exist is a different, spatial statement — that ninety-five percent of an
          infestation is in five percent of the house or yard — which we suspect is where the confusion
          started. We are not going to repeat a statistic we cannot source, even a convenient one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What is well supported is the concentration.</strong> The population is where the animal
          rests, not where it walks. The dog's bed, the spot under the coffee table, the shaded patch by
          the back step, the gap under the porch, the cat's chair. That is where eggs fall, where larvae
          find the organic debris they need, and where cocoons end up.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The species, briefly.</strong> It is almost certainly the cat flea regardless of what
          animal you own — UC IPM calls it the most common ectoparasite on domestic cats and dogs, and
          Texas A&amp;M's livestock entomologists list its hosts as cats, humans, domestic dogs, several
          livestock species, opossums and raccoons. That last part matters more than it looks: the same
          insect on your dog is at home on the wildlife crossing your yard at night.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Vacuuming, which is a treatment step and not housework</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>It provokes the stage that cannot be sprayed.</strong> Kentucky states that vacuuming
          stimulates pre-adult fleas to emerge sooner from their insecticide-resistant cocoons, hastening
          their contact with insecticide residues. UC IPM says the same, describing vacuuming as effective
          at killing larvae in carpet, picking up adults, and stimulating pre-emerged adults to leave their
          cocoons.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>So the timing is deliberate.</strong> Vacuum before the treatment, and keep vacuuming
          afterwards. You are trying to bring the protected stage out into an environment that will kill
          it. Utah State's guidance makes the same point — that the vibration triggers emergence and should
          be done prior to insecticide application.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>How much it removes on its own.</strong> The sources conflict. AgriLife gives 15 to 30
          percent of larvae and 30 to 60 percent of flea eggs from carpeting with a good beater-type
          vacuum. Ohio State says frequent vacuuming has been shown to remove up to 95 percent of flea
          eggs, some larvae and adults. UC IPM calls it very effective without a number. We quote
          AgriLife's figure because it is the most conservative and the most specific, and we are telling
          you the others exist.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Do it where it counts.</strong> The resting places, under furniture, along edges and
          skirting, and the pet's bedding — which is worth washing hot rather than vacuuming. A general
          pass over the middle of open floor is the least productive version of this.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And keep doing it.</strong> Every day or two through the emergence window is the useful
          cadence, not once on treatment day. The whole point is that the population is arriving in
          instalments over several weeks.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The yard, and why most of it does not need treating</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Sunlight is lethal to the immature stages.</strong> Oklahoma State states that open areas
          of lawn exposed to continuous sunlight will not support flea development, but shady moist areas
          will. UC IPM says larvae are unlikely to survive where there is sun exposure or substantial foot
          traffic.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Which makes blanket yard spraying poor value.</strong> Georgia's extension service says
          it outright: since larvae prefer shaded areas with high humidity and limited air movement,
          spraying the entire yard is wasteful. AgriLife's guidance is to focus outdoor treatment on crawl
          spaces, bedding areas, sites under decks and shrubbery, and shady areas where pets spend a lot of
          time.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>In this climate that narrows things down sharply.</strong> An open West Texas lawn in
          July, in full sun, with humidity in the teens, is about as hostile an environment as a flea larva
          could be given. The habitat here is specific and small: under the porch, under the deck, the
          shaded north side of the house, the crawl space, the dog run under a tree, the gap behind the
          shed, the shaded patch where the animal actually lies down.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Sometimes no outdoor treatment is needed at all.</strong> UC IPM notes that treating the
          pet with any of the preferred products normally will prevent reinfestation. That is worth knowing
          before anybody quotes you a yard programme.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The reservoir question.</strong> UC IPM notes feral cats, opossums, squirrels and coyotes
          can carry fleas and advises limiting food, water and shelter for them. Texas DSHS makes the same
          point in the context of disease, advising that yards be kept so that rodents, opossums and stray
          cats cannot live there. On acreage and on the edge of these towns, that outside reservoir is
          often the reason a house keeps being re-seeded after everything indoors was done correctly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Disease, kept in proportion</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Flea-borne typhus is real and it is increasing in Texas.</strong> DSHS reports over 8,600
          cases between 2008 and 2025, with a low of 135 in 2010 and a high of 1,110 in 2025.
          Approximately 72 percent of cases were hospitalised, and 17 deaths have been attributed to the
          disease. Symptoms are fever, headache, chills, weakness, nausea and vomiting, body aches and
          rash, with an incubation period of six to fourteen days.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And it is not happening here.</strong> DSHS describes most Texas cases occurring in South
          Texas, from Nueces County southward to the Rio Grande Valley, with newer endemic areas emerging
          in the Dallas–Fort Worth metroplex, greater Houston, Bexar and Travis counties. In the DSHS
          county table covering 2008 to 2019, the only West Texas county appearing at all is El Paso, with
          a single case in 2013. The highest-burden counties in that same table are Hidalgo with 1,053,
          Nueces with 667, Bexar with 425 and Cameron with 388.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why we are publishing both halves.</strong> A pest control company quoting the 8,600 and
          stopping there would be doing something we think is dishonest. The disease is worth knowing about
          — particularly if you travel to South Texas, or if somebody in the house has a fever and a rash
          that nobody can place — and it is not a reason to be frightened of a flea on the Llano Estacado.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>One mechanism worth understanding.</strong> The CDC explains that fleas defecate when
          they feed, that this flea dirt contains the bacteria, and that it can be rubbed into the bite
          wound or another wound and cause infection. So transmission is not really the bite — it is the
          scratching afterwards. That is a genuinely useful thing to know and it is not what most people
          assume.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What we will tell you before we start</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>That you will still see fleas.</strong> Kentucky's expectation — a few weeks or longer —
          is what we will say up front rather than after you ring back annoyed. Every one you see in that
          window is a newly emerged adult from a cocoon that nothing could have reached.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>That four weeks is the threshold.</strong> Kentucky puts retreatment on the table when
          fleas continue appearing beyond four weeks. That is a specific, published line, and it is the one
          we work to rather than an arbitrary schedule.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>That the pet's treatment is your vet's business, not ours.</strong> The animal has to be
          on something appropriate and it has to stay on it, and that conversation belongs to somebody
          qualified to have it. A house treatment against an untreated animal is a losing arrangement and
          we will say so rather than take the work.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>That the vacuuming is genuinely part of the treatment.</strong> Not an afterthought and
          not tidying — it is the step that brings the protected stage out where the treatment can act on
          it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And that we will not sell you a whole-yard programme</strong> when three extension
          services say the sunlit part of a yard does not support flea development. The shaded resting
          places are the target. The rest of the lawn is doing the work for us. More of that reasoning is
          on the <a href="/services/flea-and-tick/">flea and tick page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <Faq items={faqs} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta
    heading="Fleas that will not finish?"
    body="Tell us when the pet was treated, when the house was treated, and whether the property stood empty at any point. Those three answers usually explain the timing before anybody drives out."
  />
</BaseLayout>
